{"type":"thinking","text":"Need design. We'll make detailed stylized pelican on bicycle, side view, animated wheels/cranks. Must ensure \"pelican actively riding a bicycle\", sits saddle. Need likely foreground landscape perhaps nice. Need animated SVG (SMIL). We can create with high quality. Should we involve subagent? Not needed. We need maybe render frames to inspect.\n\nLet's plan composition 900x700 maybe. Pelican riding bicycle side profile facing right. Bicycle with two wheels, frame, handlebars, saddle. Pelican body sitting upright on saddle, feet on pedals, wings maybe one or both reaching handlebars. Big beak with pouch, eye, feather details. Animation: wheels rotate via spokes, crank rotation; pedals remain horizontal? Could animate crank group rotation with pedals counter-rotating to remain level, and legs as groups perhaps multiple positions not feasible with SMIL? We can animate legs using path morphing or rotate thigh/shank. Simpler: pelican legs bend with animation via motion of foot attached to rotating pedal. The feet can move with pedals (position on circle). Upper leg from body to knee / webbed foot. To be convincing, animate leg via path d attribute values maybe SMIL animate path d. Or use transforms on thigh/shin? We can create leg group at hip and animate shin? More complex but doable.\n\nNeed \"actively riding\" - wings on handlebar, legs pedaling, body bob, neck bob, maybe scarf flutter. We can have near crank and far crank. Pedals rotate but counter-rotate to stay horizontal. Attach feet to pedal approximate. Pelican feet orange, animated positions. If we want legs follow pedals, use paths with animate values matching crank angle positions. Could animate leg as two-segment path through keyframes; but visual quality attainable.\n\nLet's define coordinate system. SVG 960x720. Ground at y=600. Bicycle rear wheel center (315, 495), front wheel center (690, 495), radius 105. Frame:\n- rear axle 315,495\n- bottom bracket 505,505? Radius of crank ~42. \n- seat tube to saddle (455,355)\n- head tube top (665,355), bottom (690,410)\n- chainstay rear axle to BB.\n- seat stay rear axle to seat top.\n- down tube BB to head lower.\n- top tube seat top to head top.\n- fork head bottom to front axle.\n- handlebar around 680,320.\nWheels r=100 maybe ground y=600. Hub at 315, 500 and 690,500; rim radius 100 (tire) so bottom 600. Let's set.\n\nBicycle side profile facing right. Pelican sits saddle around x 455 y 335. Body centered ~450,300, big white body, wing to handlebar around x650 y330. Neck up to head x565 y190, beak extends right to x780 y230 with pouch. Needs legs from body underside around 470,365 to pedals at crank x510 +/- 40 and y505 +/- crank radius, close. Pelican legs maybe relatively long webbed feet. Far leg can be behind frame.\n\nTo convey active riding:\n- wheels rotate continuously 2.5s per rev maybe 2s, spokes.\n- crank rotates 1.25s per rev, pedals counter rotate.\n- legs and feet animate with phase. One leg near (orange) follows pedal. Upper leg origin around hip (460,350)? Pelican body squat. Foot on pedal same y around 490, x from 470 to 550. Leg length maybe too short. Need pelican leg lengths maybe ~120. Hip at (450,380), foot around (505,480). The crank may overstretch. Can set hip at (470,385) and crank center 500,475 with radius 35. Feet orbit from x465-535, y440-510. Distance from hip ~55-125. Fine. Body bottom ~380. Add leg segments.\n\nLet's design drawing style: clean vector, illustrative, maybe teal/mustard retro palette. Background sky gradient, sun, clouds, distant hills, road. Bicycle maybe dark ink outlines, wheels cream. Pelican white/off-white with shading, orange beak and legs, blue vest? Maybe scarf for action. Animation should be noticeable.\n\nCould make a fun pelican with helmet? User asks pelican riding bicycle, saddle. A bird wearing a little red scarf and maybe cycling cap. Pelican body white, beak orange. \"actively\" show determined expression and wind lines.\n\nNeed not overcomplicate. Need animate:\n- wheel spin group including rim? Rotate whole wheel spokes but tire static maybe. Rather rotate spokes/hub, keep tire.\n- crank spins.\n- pedals counter-rotate (using animateTransform rotate around pedal center? If pedal group nested inside crank at point and rotates with crank, to keep horizontal apply counter rotation to inner group around its own location. Structure: outer crank rotating around BB; at crank end, a circle for pedal axle and a pedal group with animateTransform rotate -360 around pedal's local center. But when nested under rotating group, transform order with local rotation around point. We can place pedal group at (radius,0), and counter-rotate around (0,0) inside. However rotating outer also moves. It stays horizontal as total outer rotation + inner counter rotation = 0. Fine. Need use from 0 to -360, same dur on outer 360.\n\n- legs maybe animated using paths and keyframes. Could use animate attributeName=\"d\" with values at 0%,25%,50%,75%,100%. We can handcraft leg path at four crank positions. Alternative draw near thigh from hip to knee and shin from knee to ankle, transform groups rotating; harder align foot. We could animate foot position along crank orbit via animateMotion, and leg path morph. Hmm.\n\nMaybe simplify legs as shape using path animation with 4 keyframes. Hip origin fixed. Need two legs: far leg (behind bike, lighter) phase shifted 180°. At crank angles for near pedal: 0deg = right, 90 = down, 180 left, 270 up. We can draw upper leg from hip to knee. A bird leg has ankle maybe. Pelican foot orange webbed. We can create a leg path from hip through knee to foot and give animated d. The foot shape attached? Better use group for each leg containing path with animated d then foot group animate positions. SMIL can animate transform translate for foot, but also matching.\n\nMaybe easier: legs as two line segments with path d morph. On foot, create webbed foot at coordinates. Animate foot group via animateTransform translate values along pedal positions. Need ensure foot on pedal. The pedal positions can be computed. We can draw foot as a webbed triangular shape centered at 0,0, then translate to pedal/anchor.\n\nCould also animate knee positions. Let's set:\nCrank center (505,485), radius 38.\nNear pedal positions:\nphase 0: (543,485)\nphase .25: (505,523)\nphase .5: (467,485)\nphase .75: (505,447).\nHip position around (463,373), leg lengths: to foot at 543,485 distance 137; to 467,485 distance 112; down 505,523 distance 156; up 505,447 distance 86. This is a bit variable. In real pedaling, leg length is constant but hip above, foot on circle; knee bends. Fine. Hip maybe 470,390. Distances 128,104,139,78. Maybe crank radius 32 and hip 465,375: foot x 473-537, y 453-517; distances around ~100-147. Could extend body bottom lower around 390. Pelican body large, hip around 438? Let's think proportions.\n\nComposition: pelican body broad oval centered 450,300, width 240 height 220 maybe. Saddle at 440,355. Pelican sitting on saddle; legs emerge from lower body around x470,y385. Crank at 500,490. Leg length 100. Radius 34. Fine.\n\nNear leg bent knee maybe outward right? Usually knee forward/right. Hip (455,380); knee lies forward and up about x500,y415; foot at pedal positions. Path: M455,380 Q490,405 500,435 L foot. Hmm.\n\nInstead use two segments that can bend: hip->knee->foot. For each phase, choose knee:\n- foot right (543,490): knee (515,413)\n- foot down (505,524): knee (537,450)\n- foot left (467,490): knee (512,438)\n- foot up (505,456): knee (518,414)\nThen ankle/foot at pedal. But smooth animation between keyframes can cross pleasantly. Need phase align with near crank. We'll create path values at 0%, 25%, 50%, 75%, 100%. Use animate attributeName d dur 1.2s repeatCount indefinite calcMode spline? SMIL additive? Values with semicolons and keyTimes. The path d values must have same command structure. e.g. M 458 382 C 480 395 500 410 512 435 C 518 448 530